The PDP-11 emulator on Debian
===============

Run qunilator-setup and you are done:

    sudo qunilator-setup

It settles the boot configuration, checks that the PRUs and the cape are
there, bridges the uplink so the emulated machine can reach the network,
tells the emulated Ethernet controller which interface to bind to, and
starts the services. It prints the address to point a browser at.

Running it again is harmless. If a bridge already carries the uplink it is
kept as it is, rather than rebuilt.

On a kernel newer than 4.19 the first run usually changes /boot/uEnv.txt and
stops there, because those settings only take effect at boot:

    sudo qunilator-setup      # applies boot settings, asks for a reboot
    sudo reboot
    sudo qunilator-setup      # finishes

The original file is kept as /boot/uEnv.txt.bone-orig.

Boot settings
-------------

bone_capemgr was removed in 4.19, so the cape overlay no longer loads from
the cape's own EEPROM and U-Boot applies it instead. qunilator-setup names
QBone.dtbo in a free overlay slot, and turns off three overlays that would
otherwise claim the cape's pins first - the eMMC's, HDMI's and the audio
codec's. It also comments out uboot_overlay_pru: that overlay names a
PRU-ICSS compatible no driver has bound since 6.10, so applying it takes the
PRUs away rather than providing them.

Beyond the cape's pins, QBone.dtbo declares the memory the emulated machine
runs in and the interrupt the PRUs raise. uio_pdrv_genirq binds to the
latter only when told which compatible to look for, which
/etc/modprobe.d/bone.conf does.

If qunilator-setup reports a missing piece it names which one, and each maps to
one of these settings.

qunilator-setup runs in two passes. The first applies the boot settings, builds
the legacy-Ethernet device tree and writes the bridge configuration; it then
asks for a reboot, because both the device tree and the bridge take effect
only at boot. The second, after the reboot, finds everything in place and
starts the services. Run it, reboot when it says to, and run it again.

The distribution image does this without a login: qunilator-setup.service runs
"qunilator-setup --auto", which reboots between the passes itself and records
/var/lib/qunilator/.setup-done when the network is configured, so it runs only
until the board is set up. The unit ships disabled on a package install,
where the operator runs qunilator-setup by hand; the image enables it.

Moving eth0 onto the bridge moves this board's address onto the bridge, and
the device tree swap changes the Ethernet driver -- either could put a
headless board out of reach if something is wrong. There is no live rollback:
the changes are applied by the reboot, so keep a serial console to hand the
first time. The stock device tree is kept as
/boot/dtbs/<kernel>/am335x-boneblack-uboot.dtb.stock; restoring it and
rebooting returns eth0 to its original driver.

To skip the network entirely:

    sudo qunilator-setup --no-network

The emulated machine can then reach this board, which is enough for a local
mopd, but not the rest of the network.

What it sets up
---------------

The emulated Ethernet controller needs a promiscuous interface that carries
the guest's several MAC addresses and reaches both the LAN and this board at
layer 2 (mopd boots the guest over Ethernet). That is a Linux bridge over a
plain NIC. The stock 6.x kernel binds the switchdev cpsw driver, whose eth0
is a hardware switch port the kernel will not add to a software bridge, so
qunilator-setup builds a device tree that binds the legacy ti,cpsw driver and
installs it as the DTB U-Boot loads.

The bridge itself is systemd-networkd: br0 carries eth0 (the uplink) and
veth-br (the controller's veth), and holds this board's DHCP address, pinned
to the uplink's MAC so the lease does not change. qunilator-network creates the
veth pair from /etc/qunilator/network.conf before the emulator starts; the
controller binds to veth-pdp. The networkd files live in
/etc/systemd/network/{br0.netdev,br0.network,eth0.network,veth-br.network,
veth-pdp.network,usb0.network}.

A machine plugged into the board's USB port reaches it too.
qunilator-usb-gadget builds the gadget over configfs, and one cable carries two
ways in:

    CDC Ethernet          usb0, which usb0.network fixes at 192.168.7.2 and
                          which serves the attached host a lease on the same
                          subnet - so http://192.168.7.2/ answers with no LAN,
                          no DHCP server and no address to discover
    CDC ACM               /dev/ttyGS0, a serial console carrying a login, which
                          answers when the network is the thing that is broken

A Mac or a Linux host binds both with no driver of its own. The address is
configured whether or not anything is plugged in, which is what lets the login
banner name it. The getty waits on the serial port for a host to arrive, and a
drop-in has agetty open that port itself with CLOCAL: the port carries a
carrier signal only while a host is attached, and the stock unit's own setup of
the port waits on that carrier in the kernel, where nothing can interrupt it -
which leaves a getty systemd cannot stop and a shutdown that never finishes.

The gadget is built once and left standing - stopping the service leaves it in
place, and starting it again over a tree that is already there is what repairs
a half-built one. Dismantling it is a manual step, and the getty on its serial
port comes off first:

    systemctl stop serial-getty@ttyGS0.service
    qunilator-usb-gadget stop

That order matters. Removing a configfs function directory while a port it
carries is open blocks in the kernel and cannot be interrupted, taking the
getty with it; qunilator-usb-gadget refuses to start dismantling while the
getty is running rather than wedge on it.

Serial ports
------------

The board brings out three UARTs. ttyS0 is on the debug header, J1 pins 4 and
5, and wants a 3.3 V TTL adapter; ttyS1 and ttyS2 come out on the cape. Each is
either a Linux login or the emulator's, and the System page of the web
interface says which. As the image ships, ttyS0 and ttyS1 carry a login - the
ttyS1 login is the QUniBone Classic arrangement - and ttyS2 is free for an
emulated DL11 or for the console bridge that carries a real CPU's own SLU.
Holding a port from the Linux side is what stops the emulator opening it, and
the interface refuses to put a login on a port an enabled device has.

One port keeps its login at all times: it is the way back onto a board whose
network has gone. All of them run at 115200 8N1, and the banner above the
prompt names the addresses the board answers on, so a terminal shows where to
point a browser without logging in.

The kernel console follows the first port that carries a login, so printk never
writes into a line an emulated device holds. That is a boot setting, console=
in /boot/uEnv.txt, so the interface says when a reboot is needed to finish the
move.

Running
-------

qunilator-setup starts and enables the services. Afterwards they behave as any
others:

    systemctl status qbone      # or unibone, on a UNIBUS board
    systemctl restart qbone

The web interface answers on port 80. State - disk images, saved
configurations and settings.json - lives in /var/lib/qunilator.

The distribution image ships a root filesystem shrunk to fit; qunilator-resize
grows it to fill the card in two stages across a reboot - extend the
partition on the first boot, grow the ext4 into it once the kernel has
re-read the table on the next - then records /var/lib/qunilator/.resized and
does not run again.

/usr/bin/qbone (/usr/bin/unibone on a UNIBUS board) serves the web interface
and has no menu. The machine a board
comes up as is a saved configuration: add "--config <name>" to the unit's
ExecStart to apply one at startup. /usr/bin/qbone-cli is the same emulation
driven from a terminal menu, for bus latch, master/slave and device exerciser
work; it needs an operator on stdin and is not what the service runs. It drives
the PRU and the bus, so it is installed set-user-id root and executable only by
the qunilator-admin group - the same membership that carries sudo and a login
shell. An operator in that group runs it directly; everyone else has the web
interface.

A board is one set of hardware, so one program drives it at a time: stop the
service before starting qbone-cli, and start it again afterwards.

The emulated Ethernet controller binds to veth-pdp, the interface
qunilator-network creates and joins to the bridge qunilator-setup builds from the
uplink. Rename it in /etc/qunilator/network.conf and the delqa's "interface"
parameter has to follow.

Machines
--------

A machine is a saved configuration and the media its drives hold. This package
ships one: the XXDP diagnostic pack as /var/lib/qunilator/images/xxdp25.rl02,
and a configuration named "xxdp" that attaches it. On a UNIBUS board that
configuration is a whole emulated PDP-11/20 with the M9312 ROMs, so it boots
the pack by itself and is the quickest check that a board works; on a QBUS
board it is the drive alone, for the real machine in the backplane to boot.

The pack is read-only and belongs to this package. A drive keeps its guest's
writes in a copy-on-write overlay beside the file, so the shipped pack stays as
it was and reinstalling cannot lose any work.

The configuration is placed only when the board has none by that name, so
editing or deleting it is a decision an upgrade will not undo.

Other machines are imported. The web interface exports a configuration and
every image it names as a single <name>.qcfg.zip, and the Configurations page
imports the same file back, so a machine travels as one file between boards.

Status LEDs
-----------

qunilator-leds drives a contiguous run of three green user LEDs - usr0, usr1,
usr2 - to show how far the board is through bring-up, and relocates SD-card
activity (the mmc0 trigger, normally usr1) to usr3, whose eMMC trigger is
idle since the board runs from the SD card. A growing blink marks progress
and a bouncing light means the emulator is up:

    X00   booting        blink
    XX0   configuring    blink
    XXX   starting       blink
    bounce               ready

The phase comes from systemd and /var/lib/qunilator/.setup-done, so the bounce
simply means the emulator's service is active. The unit is enabled on the distribution
image and shipped disabled in the package; `systemctl disable --now
qunilator-leds` returns the LEDs to the kernel on the next boot.

Updating
--------

The web interface tells you when a newer package is published, shows what
changed, and installs it. That path is the one to use: it says what an update
costs before it starts, watches the new service come up, and puts the old
version back if it does not.

qunilator-update is the same mechanism from the command line:

    qunilator-update --check       refresh the index, report installed/candidate
    qunilator-update --changelog   the candidate's changelog since installed
    qunilator-update --status      the status file the interface publishes
    qunilator-update --rollback    put the cached previous version back

It reads the apt repository /etc/apt/sources.list.d/<name>.list names, which
the appliance image configures, so apt checks the signed index and the package
hash and there is no unsigned install path. A board installed by hand with
"dpkg -i" has no such source and reports so: `apt install` from the repository
once makes it updateable.

qunilator-update-check.timer runs the check five minutes after boot and daily
after that, with a randomised delay. Nothing is ever installed without someone
asking for it, and /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/51qunilator-unattended keeps
unattended-upgrades away from the emulator package - an unattended upgrade
would stop the emulated machine with no warning.

Installing a new version restarts the service, which stops the emulated
machine. The device set is shut down in order, so the disk images are flushed,
but a running Unix or RSX has its buffer cache cut, and the board comes back up
in its startup configuration. Halt the guest first if that matters.

The updater's state lives in /var/lib/qunilator/updates, root-only: the status
file, the requested version, the staged candidate and a cached copy of the
version the board runs, which is what a rollback reinstalls.

    journalctl -u qunilator-update          an install
    journalctl -u qunilator-update-check    the daily check

The board's other packages are reported by the same check and upgraded with
"qunilator-update --os-upgrade", or from the interface. That path has no
rollback - apt cannot undo an upgrade - so it holds the emulator package back,
upgrades nothing that would remove another package, and never reboots the
board. The kernel stays held from the image build.

Building on the board
---------------------

The board builds the emulator it runs, which is what debugging on the hardware
takes: compile.sh is that build. crossbuild.sh in the same tree is the faster
path, needing Docker on a desktop rather than the board.

A flashed board carries no source, so fetch one and what it needs to compile:

    sudo apt-get install -y git build-essential libtirpc-dev
    git clone https://github.com/QUniBone/QUniLator.git
    cd QUniLator

libtirpc is Sun RPC, which the blinkenlight panel client speaks and glibc no
longer carries. The TI PRU code generation tools are already here -- the image
carries clpru as ti-pru-cgt-v2.3 -- so the PRU firmware builds too.

Name the bus this board bridges. The example file is a UniBone; a QBone swaps
the commented pair inside it:

    cp qunibone-platform.env.example qunibone-platform.env

Then build, from the top of the checkout:

    ./compile.sh              # or ./compile.sh -a to start from clean

It writes both programs into 10.03_app_demo/4_deploy_q (4_deploy_u on a UNIBUS
board): qbone-web, the service the unit runs, and demo, the same emulation
driven from a terminal menu, which the package installs as qbone-cli. Expect the
better part of an hour on a BeagleBone.

To run what was built, put it where the unit looks. Renaming onto the file
replaces it even while it is running, and the restart picks it up:

    sudo install -m 755 10.03_app_demo/4_deploy_q/qbone-web /usr/bin/qbone.new
    sudo mv /usr/bin/qbone.new /usr/bin/qbone
    sudo systemctl restart qbone

Only one program at a time drives the PRUs and the bus, so stop the service
before running demo by hand:

    sudo systemctl stop qbone
    sudo ./10.03_app_demo/4_deploy_q/demo

The operator's account
----------------------

A board that has no password answers every request, which is what makes a
freshly installed one reachable. Open the interface and it asks for a user
name and a password before anything else; from then on the browser asks for
them on every visit. The password is stored in settings.json as a
PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 digest, so that file becomes readable only by root.

That one name and password are the whole board's login. Setting them creates
an account of the same name with a home under /home, a login shell and
membership of qunilator-admin, which carries sudo - so the same credentials
reach the web interface, the SMB, FTP and SFTP shares of the image library,
and an ssh session. Paste an ssh public key into the same dialog and the
account answers that key too. Both are changeable afterwards from the System
page, which also renames the board.

The base image is onboarded with its own shared account, debian, whose
password is the same on every board of that image. Once the operator's
account exists, that password is locked - the account, its home and its
groups stay as they are, and

    sudo usermod --unlock debian

brings the password back.

Open the interface promptly after installing. Until a password is set, anyone
who can reach port 80 can operate the machine.

Setting WEBUI_PASSWORD in the service's environment overrides the web
credentials: the password is then whatever the variable says, the interface
will not change it, and no operator account is created.

The service runs as root
------------------------

Four things it does need it. The PRU driver maps physical memory through
/dev/mem, which the kernel gates on CAP_SYS_RAWIO whatever the device's mode
says. The bus engine runs at realtime scheduling priority. The web interface
administers the appliance - it creates and removes the operator's account, sets
Unix and Samba passwords, renames the board, rewrites the share configurations
and moves the serial logins - and each of those is a root operation in its own
right. The interface answers on port 80.

What follows for the image tree is that the service is not bound by its file
permissions the way another program would be: root is granted a read/write open
of any file. Where that matters - a medium the operator has write-protected -
the emulator reads the file's owner write bit and honours it rather than letting
the open decide, so "chmod 444" on an image protects it. The mode is read as the
image is attached to a drive and kept for as long as it is there.

An unprivileged service would still need CAP_SYS_RAWIO for /dev/mem, which
carries the power to write kernel memory, so the privilege it keeps is close to
the privilege it gave up; and the administration above would move into a setuid
helper, which is the same authority reached through a longer path. Reaching the
PRU memories and the emulated machine's DDR window as UIO maps declared by the
cape overlay is what would remove the capability, and it puts new regions into
the overlay that the board's whole bus access depends on.
